"lidator_active_set_cap), with session keys and\\n   bonded self-stake set at genesis and an empty queue. Queue-and-rotation admission (\u00a715.5) applies only\\n   after genesis.\\n2. **Ranking key is earned work, not stake.** Testnet stake is faucet-issued, so the cohort is ranked by\\n   verification liveness on testnet \u2014 accepted verification duties per epoch (the `validatorEpochs`\\n   signal of the testnet earnings rules) \u2014 then uptime, then earlier registration as the tie-break.\\n   Stake enters only as the bond gate at genesis.\\n3. **Placement is an admission input, capped on count and on stake.** Each candidate signs a placement\\n   declaration (hosting ASN, metro, operating entity). Genesis composition rejects any cohort in which\\n   one hosting ASN, one metro, or one operating group exceeds **10 % of the seeded set by validator\\n   count or 10 % of seeded self-stake**, or in which one entity exceeds `MaxSlotsPerEntity`. Both legs\\n   are required because the committee draw is stake-weighted while the capture bound is modelled on a\\n   uniform draw over identities (E.42); capping count alone would admit a small, heavily staked domain\\n   that the model does not cover. A higher-ranked candidate that would breach a cap yields its seat to\\n   the next-ranked candidate outside that domain. Foundation-operated seats count against the same\\n   caps. The histogram the composition produced is published with the chain spec.\\n4. **Bond grant.** Each seeded validator receives the D-0440 bond (1,200,000 FLOP) through the ECON-003\\n   grant lane and is bonded at genesis; a candidate whose account is not funded by the snapshot loses\\n   the seat to the next-ranked candidate.\\n5. **Post-genesis promotion** is stake-ordered among floor-passing queue entries (D-0416) and fills only\\n   free slots under the cap (D-0437). First-come promotion at the minimum stake would be withdrawn.\\n\\n**Ratified constraints (on ratification).** The ranking key, the two-part per-domain cap, and \\\"genesis\\nseeds directly, rotation thereafter\\\" become \u00a715.2/\u00a715.5 text with R-IDs; both cap legs are recorded in\\n`params/flop-protocol-params.yaml` as reference parameters until the composition tool enforces them.\\n\\n**Precedent, and the argument against this decision.** No production chain seeds mainnet genesis seats\\nby testnet rank. Cosmos Game of Stakes ranked participants primarily on precommit uptime and jail\\nhistory, explicitly not on accumulated stake, but it awarded prizes; Hub genesis remained\\nstake-determined. NEAR's Stake Wars gated *entry* to a restricted mainnet whose foundation-run nodes\\nwere progressively replaced. The softer shape \u2014 seed from foundation plus a qualified cohort, admit the\\nrest permissionlessly \u2014 carries less risk than a ranked allocation of 1,000 seats, because a published\\nranking is a farming target. Two known failure modes argue the same way: Game of Stakes saw automated\\nmetric-farming inflate the scoreboard, and NEAR's Stake Wars III kicked chunk-only producers on a\\nmetric that measured the wrong role's duty. This decision keeps the ranked form because FLOP's genesis\\ngrants the bond (D-0440) and therefore cannot be permissionless at genesis, but the ratifying reviewer\\nshould weigh the softer alternative explicitly.\\n\\n**Known weakness.** Ranking by earned work rewards Sybil farming across identities, and the bound that\\nshould contain it \u2014 `MaxSlotsPerEntity` \u2014 is self-declared and, today, read nowhere in the pallet. The\\nplacement declaration has the same problem: a declaration with no penalty attached is decoration.\\nRatifying this decision **MUST** come with a misdeclaration penalty (ejection or slash) and enforcement\\nof the slot limit, or the caps are advisory.\\n\\n**Numbers are gated.** The seeded-set size, the 10 % legs and the bond path **MUST NOT** be fixed by\\nthis record. The launch plan seats 20\u201350 then at most 100 testnet validators, so a 1,000-seat\\ncomposition has no cohort to draw from yet, and the decision cannot execute above 200 seats until E.41\\ncloses. The ranking key and the two-part cap are the ratifiable parts; the constants follow the cohort\\nunder #1713.\\n\\n**Alternatives rejected.** Stake-ranked genesis (ranks faucet balances); queue-only fill (600 days);\\nauction slots (removed by D-0421); a per-country rather than per-ASN cap (provider \u2260 country; two\\noperators in one OVH site are one fault domain).\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5, E.41. **Rationale.**\\nvalidator-set-diversity \u00a73\u2013\u00a74,\\ntestnet-to-mainnet-conversion.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0508 \u2014 Rotation on underperformance: floor-based ejection and on-chain performance signal\\n\\n**Status:** proposal, pending ratification. Wiring task #1711; models\\n`validator-rotation.qnt` (variant A = shipped\\nrule, variant B = this decision) and\\n`validator-score-provenance.qnt`.\\n\\n**Context.** SPEC-021 as shipped: a composite score (40 % uptime, 30 % block rate, 20 % accuracy,\\n10 % latency) written by a **root-origin** `update_performance` oracle, 20 % decay per rotation, ranking\\nby (recent verified work, score), a **fixed** bottom-[`validators_to_eject`](../yellowpaper.md#param-validators_to_eject) = 50\\nejected every 30 days, 7-day cooldown, `rejoin` with stake topped up. Three defects: the score is an\\noff-chain trust input; a fixed count evicts healthy validators when the whole set performs; and 50 per\\n30 days drains a failing cohort at 5 % of a 1,000 set per month. \\\"Accuracy\\\" and \\\"latency\\\" have no\\noperational definition.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Ejection is floor-based, and bounded.** A validator is eligible for ejection only if it fails the\\n   floor: no accepted verification duty within `WorkRecencyWindow` (R15.4c), **or** composite score below\\n   `performance_floor` (proposed 5,000 of 10,000). Rotation ejects floor-failing validators, lowest score\\n   first, up to a per-rotation cap of **10 % of the active set** (100 of 1,000) and promotes into the\\n   freed slots. A validator above the floor with recent verified work is never ejected by rotation.\\n\\n   Three guards make a wrong floor survivable, and they matter more than the floor value itself:\\n\\n   - **No evidence, no ejection.** A validator with fewer than a minimum number of scored samples in the\\n     window is not ejectable. This one predicate covers the bootstrap case (a fresh chain where every\\n     score is zero), the quiet-chain case (duty signals exist only when there is traffic, which is why\\n     R15.4c mandates protocol-issued challenges), and freshly promoted validators, which today enter with\\n     `performance_score = 0`.\\n   - **Minimum-set guard on every removal path.** Rotation, jail and kick **MUST NOT** reduce the active\\n     set below a minimum bound, and **MUST** skip rather than partially apply an ejection that would.\\n     Without it the shipped rule removes the entire set on any network of 50 or fewer validators\\n     (defect #1727): block production continues on stale authorities while the finality committee, which\\n     derives from the active set, empties and AlephBFT stops. That defect is open (#1727, E.56); the\\n     guard is the same predicate and may land ahead of the rest of this decision.\\n   - **The per-rotation cap is fixed, not tunable.** It is the blast-radius bound that makes a wrong floor\\n     or a mis-derived signal recoverable, so it does not become a governance knob.\\n2. **The score is derived on chain.** The four components are redefined as observable signals and the\\n   root-origin oracle is retired once they land: *uptime* = BABE slots authored / slots assigned over the\\n   interval (measurable for the whole active set); *participation* = seated AlephBFT sessions in which the\\n   validator contributed units before session close, reported by `pallet_aleph` (each validator on a\\n   1,000 pool is seated in about 10 % of sessions, roughly 290 samples per 30 days at 900-block sessions,\\n   about 72 at 3,600); *duty* = accepted verification duties plus known-answer challenges answered, over\\n   challenges issued (R15.4c); *lag* = median DAG-head distance while seated (the AlephBFT performance\\n   metric). The 40/30/20/10 weights are kept provisionally for these four in that order.\\n\\n   **The lag component carries a grace band.** Scoring distance from the DAG frontier penalises honest but\\n   geographically distant validators, which is in direct tension with D-0507's placement caps: a metric\\n   that rewards proximity pushes operators into one metro. The lag component **MUST** therefore apply no\\n   penalty within a grace band sized to worst-case honest round-trip time, and score only beyond it. This\\n   is the shape Solana adopted for the same problem in SIMD-0033 (Timely Vote Credits), which sizes its\\n   grace from the roughly 100 ms maximum latency between two points on Earth and states the goal\\n   explicitly: a strict schedule \\\"would encourage all validators to move to the same country\\\". The band is\\n   measured in the wiring task, not guessed here.\\n3. **What ejection means** is unchanged: seat, rewards, and committee eligibility are lost for at least\\n   the [`ejection_cooldown_blocks`](../yellowpaper.md#param-ejection_cooldown_blocks) window; stake is\\n   thawed, not slashed. Liveness slashing (\u00a715.7) is a separate path and may precede ejection.\\n4. **Score decay** (20 % per rotation) is retained so the floor reflects recent behaviour. Because\\n   decay runs before the floor test, a seat below `floor / 0.8` (6,250 at a 5,000 floor) re-enters the\\n   below-floor cohort at the next rotation; the floor is therefore evaluated on the pre-decay score of\\n   the rotation that ejects.\\n5. **Promoted validators keep their queued score.** The shipped `do_rotate` inserts promotions with\\n   `performance_score = 0`, which under a floor rule would make every fresh promotion eviction-eligible\\n   at the next rotation; promotion carries the candidate's queue score forward.\\n6. **Three declared constants are wired.** `EjectionCooldownBlocks`, `ScoreDecayPerRotation`, and\\n   `MaxSlotsPerEntity` are declared in the pallet `Config` and read nowhere; the Quint witnesses\\n   `witness_A_cooldown_bypass` (re-register in the same block), `witness_A_entity_overshoot` (six slots\\n   against a five-slot limit), and `witness_A_popped_candidate_discarded` (an under-staked popped\\n   candidate is dropped from both sets) show the consequences. All three become enforced predicates in\\n   the same change as the floor.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** D-0416's second leg (\\\"eject below-floor, then rotate out the bottom-N by\\nstake of the remainder\\\") would be withdrawn: ejection is floor-only, and stake remains the *promotion* key.\\nThe Quint invariant `inv_no_healthy_eviction` (I6) is stated for that reading.\\n\\n**Ratified constraints (on ratification).** R15.5 is reworded from \\\"eject the bottom 50\\\" to the floor\\nrule; `performance_floor` and the per-rotation cap enter the parameter table; the Quint invariants\\nI1\u2013I8 of `validator-rotation.qnt` hold for the wired rule (I1, I2, I3, I5 are additionally\\nmodel-checked by Apalache to depth 4; the rest by simulation).\\n\\n**What the models do and do not cover.** `validator-rotation.qnt` models the rotation **decision\\nrule** \u2014 the ejection predicate, promotion ordering, active-set cap, ejection cooldown and per-entity\\nslot limit \u2014 but deliberately does **not** model score provenance: in both variants\\n`performance_score` enters through the root `update_performance` oracle. The companion\\n`validator-score-provenance.qnt`\\ncovers decision point 2: it accumulates observable BABE / Aleph / duty / DAG counters, derives the\\n40/30/20/10 composite at rotation, resets the measurement window, protects the zero-challenge duty\\nfloor, and witnesses that an arbitrary oracle write breaks the derivation invariant. The artifacts\\nremain deliberately separate: the provenance model does not model ejection or promotion, and the\\nrotation model still treats its score input as given.\\n\\n**Precedent.** Aleph Zero, the production deployment of this consensus family, already runs an absolute\\nfloor with hysteresis on exactly these two signals: it suspends a validator after more than 48 sessions\\nbelow 80 % of expected blocks, or more than 24 sessions more than 11 units behind the DAG frontier, with\\ncounters reset every 10 eras. The counter-with-reset shape is preferred to a single-rotation threshold,\\nfor the same reason as the guards above: it survives a transient outage. The wider trend separates\\nejection from slashing \u2014 Cosmos Hub has an open proposal to remove its downtime slash outright and\\nOsmosis runs without one. D-0409's 1 % jail and 5 % kick are not revisited here; the interaction is\\nflagged for the ratifying reviewer.\\n\\n**Numbers are gated, principles are not.** The floor value, the sample minimum, the grace band and the\\nweights **MUST NOT** be fixed by this record: they need the score distribution of a real cohort, which no\\nnetwork has produced. This decision ratifies the shape \u2014 floor rather than bottom-N, derived rather than\\noracular, bounded blast radius, no ejection without evidence. The constants are set after one or two\\nrotations of testnet data, under #1711.\\n\\n**Alternatives rejected.** Keep the fixed bottom-N (evicts healthy nodes, and at 50 per 30 days cannot\\ndrain a bad cohort); keep the root oracle (a single key decides who is ejected); rank by stake for\\nejection (the spec already forbids padding the liveness signal to buy rank, and stake is the *promotion*\\nkey, not the *ejection* key); a floor relative to the set's median (re-opens the tournament dynamic\\nD-0416 removed, and mass-ejects when a network-wide incident moves the median).\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a715.4, \u00a715.5. **Rationale.**\\nvalidator-set-diversity \u00a71, SPEC-021, D-0416, D-0437.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0509 \u2014 Committee session cadence aligned to the BABE epoch\\n\\n**Status:** proposal, pending ratification. Consensus safety zone: wiring task #1712 needs CTO\\nratification and a devnet handover measurement before the constant flips.\\n\\n**Context.** `AlephEpochBlocks` = 900 (~15 min) while the BABE epoch is 3,600 blocks. The committee\\nseed is BABE epoch randomness, fixed for the epoch and one epoch old (R15.4b), so the four sessions\\ninside one BABE epoch are predictable together: a 15-minute session does not shorten the\\nadaptive-corruption window below the epoch. The Lean model prices the cadence directly: on the finite\\n1,000 pool at 900 blocks the yearly union bound is 5.7e-8 at 10 % attacker concentration and 1.3e-2 at\\n15 %; at 3,600 blocks the 10 % bound is 1.4e-8 and the binomial 15 % bound falls from 6.44e-2 to 1.61e-2\\n(`CommitteeSampling.pool_p10_year_capture_le_5_7e_minus_8`, `pool_p10_hourly_refresh_capture_le_1_43e_minus_8`,\\n`p15HourlyRefreshRisk`). Shorter sessions buy faster dilution of a bad draw and more scoring samples; they\\ncost three extra committee handovers per epoch and four times the union-bound terms. Aleph Zero runs\\n900-block sessions with a ~14-seat finality committee; FLOP's 100 seats make handover the larger cost.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. `AlephEpochBlocks` = `EpochDurationInBlocks` (3,600 in production, 60 in fast/dev builds). Committee\\n   refresh coincides with the BABE epoch boundary; the domain-separated seed (R15.4b) is unchanged.\\n2. The live Lean profile becomes the 3,600-block draw count (`epochYearDraws 3600` = 8,760) and\\n   `scripts/check_lean_constants.py` continues to tie `AlephEpochBlocks` to it. The seed-correlation\\n   note on `draws_union_bound` already sizes `AdaptiveCorruptionBoundary.epochDuration` against the BABE\\n   epoch rather than `AlephEpochBlocks`.\\n3. Scoring under D-0508 is re-based on about 2.4 seatings per validator per day (about 72 per 30-day\\n   rotation on a 1,000 pool), which the decision records as adequate.\\n4. Before the constant flips: measure session-handover cost (keychain rebuild, DAG catch-up) at a\\n   100-seat committee on the devnet and record it in\\n   validator-set-diversity.\\n\\n**Precedent.** Every documented beacon-driven committee design derives all committees of a period from\\none seed fixed before that period begins: Ethereum computes an epoch's committees from a RANDAO mix two\\nepochs old (`MIN_SEED_LOOKAHEAD`, with `MAX_SEED_LOOKAHEAD` bounding activation grinding), and Cardano\\nfixes the epoch nonce and stake snapshot in the preceding epoch. FLOP's 900-block session inside a\\n3,600-block seed period is the inverse of that pattern and buys no unpredictability. We found no paper\\nnaming this as an error directly, so the argument rests on the uniformity of the precedent rather than a\\ncitation.\\n\\n**Alternatives rejected.** Keep 900 (buys bad-draw dilution only; the seed already fixes committees for\\nthe epoch); 28,800 (8 h; a validator is seated 0.3\u00d7/day, too few samples for rotation scoring, and a bad\\ndraw lives eight hours); a per-session fresh seed (a different mechanism, E.42).\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a72.2, \u00a715.4, parameter table. **Rationale.** `CommitteeSampling.lean`\\n(`epochYearDraws_antitone`), validator-set-diversity \u00a71.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0510 \u2014 Bootstrap nodes and the public discovery plane\\n\\n**Status:** proposal, pending ratification. Operational, not a runtime change; the launch checklist item\\nis P-11 in `docs/TESTNET_MAINNET_LAUNCH_PLAN.md` and the procedure is\\n`docs/setup/bootnodes.md`.\\n\\n**Context.** On stock Substrate a bootnode is only a peer-discovery seed. On FLOP it is load-bearing:\\n`aleph_reserved_from_network()` (`pallets/relay-node/service/src/aleph_support.rs`) unions the CLI\\n`--reserved-nodes` with the chain spec's `bootNodes`, and that union is the reserved peer set of both\\nAleph notification protocols on every node, retained across committee rotations, with 50 peer slots\\nabove the committee cap held for archive/RPC/bootnodes (PR #743). A `bootNodes` entry is therefore a\\npermanent dial target for finality gossip, and a bootnode outage degrades the validator clique's seed,\\nnot just fresh-node discovery. Today the only bootnode is `alice` inside the devnet compose, no public\\nFLOP bootnode, RPC, or telemetry host exists, `flop_simnet` and `flop_devnet` share `protocol_id =\\n\\\"flop\\\"` (a cross-network peering hazard), and P-01 (\\\"testnet chain spec generated\\\") has no bootnode\\ninput although the spec must carry the multiaddrs.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. **Bootnodes are a launch requirement**, cut before the chain spec: testnet \u2265 2 on \u2265 2 hosting ASNs\\n   and \u2265 2 continents; mainnet \u2265 3 on \u2265 3 hosting ASNs and \u2265 2 continents, IPv4 and IPv6, behind DNS\\n   names with persisted node keys (`zkv-relay key generate-node-key --file`, `--node-key-file`,\\n   mode 0400).\\n2. **A bootnode is not a validator** and carries no session keys; it is a non-validating full node on a\\n   modest profile, never co-located with a validator, and it counts toward the same 10 % per-domain\\n   placement cap as validators ([D-0507](#d-0507--post-testnet-genesis-validator-selection-and-placement-admission)).\\n   Validator IPs are not published.\\n3. **Each network has a unique `protocol_id`** before any public bootnode exists.\\n4. **Retirement** keeps a retired bootnode reachable for one deprecation window after the chain spec or\\n   docs drop it, because running nodes keep dialing it as a reserved peer.\\n5. **A community bootnode list** with a submission process complements the foundation set; the public\\n   RPC and telemetry endpoints follow the same placement rule as the discovery plane.\\n\\n**Known cost.** Chain-spec bootnode lists are a standing maintenance burden: Polkadot's RFC-0008 opens\\nby noting that every add or remove requires updating every chain spec, and its issue tracker carries\\nrecurring wrong-address, peer-ID-mismatch and upgrade-ban failures. That is the argument for keeping the\\nfoundation list small and stable, letting DHT discovery carry the rest, and treating the community list\\nas additive rather than load-bearing. It is also why retirement keeps a deprecation window (point 4):\\nrunning nodes hold these addresses as reserved peers.\\n\\n**Alternatives rejected.** No bootnodes (DHT-only discovery; the Aleph reserved seed would be empty on\\nfresh nodes); bootnodes on validator hosts (publishes validator IPs and couples the discovery plane to\\nthe committee's fault domains); a single provider (the Aleph Zero public plane is OVH-majority, which is\\nthe pattern this decision avoids).\\n\\n**Specification.** `docs/setup/bootnodes.md`, `docs/setup/network-launch-readiness.md`, P-11.\\n**Rationale.** validator-set-diversity \u00a72 (public discovery\\nplane), `aleph_support.rs` reserved-peer seeding.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0511 \u2014 One canonical compute-channel transcript and receipt definition\\n\\n**Status:** draft for initial public release; specification clarification only. Runtime behavior and\\nwire bytes are unchanged.\\n\\n**Context.** R12.1b and Appendix F.3 were both normative but described different transcript leaves.\\nR12.1b restated the legacy V0 field set under the undefined name `session_id`, while F.3 defines the\\nversioned leaves accepted by the runtime, including V3's decode-policy, token-ID, TOPLOC, and timing\\nbindings. The duplicate wording could lead a current client to sign a leaf that settlement rejects. The\\nreceipt's `payable` field and the per-channel scope of `decode_policy_hash` were also implicit in runtime\\nstate rather than defined by the specification.\\n\\n**Decision.** Appendix F.3 is the sole compute-channel transcript-leaf and agent-receipt wire definition.\\nR12.1b and the informative walkthrough point to it instead of restating a preimage; `channel_id` is the\\ncanonical identifier. `ChannelDecodePolicies[channel_id]` pins one policy at open for every turn,\\nincluding a multi-agent pipeline. Receipt `payable` is the gross receipt-bound settlement amount in\\n10\u207b\u00b9\u2078 FLOP base units, before audit and forward adjustments: full reserved escrow on cooperative\\nsettlement, the R12.1d tariff on unilateral settlement, or the stored forward-contract payment when that\\noverride exists. The miner's net transfer is that amount less the audit earmark, and a forward channel\\nadditionally refunds unearned availability to the buyer. The walkthrough's cooperative `open_channel`\\nsignature matches Appendix G.1.\\n\\n**Consequences.** Current clients obtain leaf versioning, TOPLOC binding, receipt fields, policy scope,\\nand payout derivation from one normative location. Existing leaf hashes, signatures, markers, balances,\\nand settlement behavior do not change.\\n\\n**Alternatives.** Defining `session_id` as an alias would preserve two leaf definitions. Giving \u00a712.1\\nprecedence over Appendix F would make the legacy tuple authoritative and break current settlement.\\nLeaving `payable` to client inference would keep cooperative and unilateral receipt signatures\\nambiguous under partial use.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** D-0505's Appendix F profile is preserved; this entry removes the conflicting\\nR12.1b restatement and makes its already-implemented per-channel policy and payout derivations explicit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## D-0512 \u2014 Development-network exception to the paid-storage activation gate\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-25, for development networks only. Scopes D-0506 item 6;\\nevery other network stays under D-0506 item 6 and R5.4k unchanged.\\n\\n**Context.** D-0506 item 6 and R5.4k keep the paid-storage extension inactive until E.54 is ratified\\nand implemented and E.47's premises are demonstrated, with no network carve-out. The owner asked for\\nthe \u00a75.4 lifecycle (capacity reservations, service lanes and retrieval vouchers, memory heads) to be\\nbuilt and exercised on the valueless devnet ahead of E.54, so implementation and client work can run\\nagainst a live chain. Code behind a flag that no default build enables activates nothing; turning it\\non for a devnet does, which is what this decision authorizes.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. A development network (devnet, Era D in the launch plan) whose token carries no value may carry the\\n   extension before E.54 when its runtime is built with a non-default, development-only build flag\\n   (`paid-storage-unratified` in the reference implementation). No testnet or mainnet runtime, chain\\n   spec, or runtime upgrade enables that flag, and the always-on node features (such as `volta`) do not\\n   imply it. A runtime built without the flag contains no part of the extension.\\n2. The active profile carries an explicit unratified-development version (`DevnetUnratified`). Every\\n   placeholder value lives in that on-chain profile, is marked unratified, and never enters the\\n   canonical parameter file. Every call and hook still rejects an absent or unknown profile (R5.4p).\\n3. Custody audits, fault adjudication, repair, and slashing may be absent on such a network; rent\\n   released without audit acceptance carries an explicit unaudited marker instead of satisfying\\n   R5.4f's audit condition.\\n4. Nothing observed on such a network is E.54 or E.47 evidence, and no availability, pricing,\\n   compensation, or conformance claim may rest on it.\\n\\n**Supersedes by scope.** D-0506 item 6, only for development networks that meet items 1\u20134.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a75.4 R5.4k and R5.4q; Appendices E.54 and H.3.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** A separate, flag-gated extension keeps the unratified profile out of every\\nproduction build while client and service work proceeds; the profile, placement and economic\\nquestions stay with E.54 and E.47. Design basis:\\nagent-memory-storage-market.md and the\\neconomics and traffic review.\\n\\n## D-0513 \u2014 One generic SS58 prefix on every FLOP network\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-29. Replaces the SS58 wording R9.8 and R6.5a inherited\\nfrom the zkVerify fork; no earlier decision set a prefix.\\n\\n**Context.** R9.8 named zkVerify's `SS58_ZKV_PREFIX` (8741) for mainnet and \\\"a distinct prefix\\\" for\\ndevnets. The reference implementation followed it only in the release build: the devnet build, the\\nchain-spec properties, the SDKs and the explorer all render addresses with the generic Substrate\\nprefix 42. Prefix 8741 was never entered in the shared registry of SS58 prefixes\\n(`paritytech/ss58-registry`), and that registry was archived on 2026-04-16 without a successor, so a\\nFLOP-specific prefix can no longer be registered there. Current Polkadot ecosystem guidance puts\\nindependent Substrate chains on 42, and common wallets already display 42.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Every FLOP network (devnet, testnet and mainnet) uses the generic Substrate SS58 prefix 42: the\\n   runtime's `SS58Prefix`, the chain-spec `ss58Format`, and every SDK and explorer.\\n2. Networks are told apart by genesis hash, not by address encoding. Chain identifiers (CAIP-2 and\\n   CAIP-10) derive from it, and every signed extrinsic commits to it.\\n3. An off-chain statement that authorizes value, such as a genesis claim statement, must bind the\\n   network (genesis hash or chain id) itself, since an address no longer encodes it.\\n4. The metadata hash commits to the prefix, so a single release runtime serves testnet and mainnet\\n   with the same prefix.\\n\\n**Specification.** \u00a76.5 R6.5a; \u00a79.3 R9.8.\\n\\n**Rationale & sources.** Registry state: the GitHub repository `paritytech/ss58-registry` reports\\n`archived: true`; its last registration request (#246, prefixes 59 and 60) was never merged.\\nEcosystem guidance: [Unifying Polkadot ecosystem address\\nformat](https://forum.polkadot.network/t/unifying-polkadot-ecosystem-address-format/10042).\\n\\n## D-0514 \u2014 Era-T conversion rules and a fail-closed miner adjustment\\n\\n**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-09-29. No earlier decision set these rules. They were a\\ndraft in ERA_T_TO_ERA_0 and a placeholder in E.38.\\n\\n**Context.** \u00a78 specified `work-vesting` only; the path that turns Era-T activity into Era-0 airdrop\\ngrants had no normative section. D-0421, D-0438 and D-0440 fixed that genesis supply goes to\\nthree airdrop cohorts and sized each pool. They did not say what earns an allocation, how the\\nsnapshot is fixed, or what the miner performance adjustment does when its inputs are missing.\\n`pallet_airdrop_vesting` applies the plain linear curve when a miner has no mainnet score. That\\nmakes missing evidence the most favourable outcome on a money path.\\n\\n**Decision.**\\n\\n1. Conversion distributes only the existing `genesis_supply`, with no incremental emission and no\\n   allocation funded twice. Each cohort draws only from its own airdrop pool, and cohorts do not\\n   cross-subsidize. `genesis_reserve` is not an Era-T airdrop pool.\\n2. A faucet grant, sponsored bond or transferred balance creates no allocation right by itself.\\n   Held Era-T balance and stake size are not scoring terms. Agents are scored on settled\\n   compute-channel spend and miners on PoUI-verified effective-FLOP served. Completed job count and\\n   active days are not scoring terms. A fraud-flagged account receives zero allocation, with no\\n   partial credit, unless the flag is overturned before the genesis specification is locked.\\n3. Conversion reads Era-T activity at a governance-ratified, finalized snapshot height. The\\n   per-account allocation and its hash are published before the genesis specification is locked;\\n   until then the snapshot may be revoked or corrected. The snapshot records each account's cohort,\\n   activity, fraud flag and cluster identity, and the published allocation binds the inputs used to\\n   filter and score it. The finalized Era-T chain state, the allocation file, the\\n   conversion-parameters file and their hashes are the evidence of record.\\n4. The miner performance adjustment fails closed. While a Miner grant lacks either performance\\n   input, or its testnet baseline is zero, the adjustment factor is taken as zero, so the grant\\n   unlocks nothing before its end block. The end-block rule is unchanged: every grant is fully vested\\n   there.\\n\\nScore caps and aggregation, the accounting iden"
